            <ArticleTitle>Association between helicobacter pylori infection and gallbladder diseases: a retrospective study.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/jgh.14054</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The association between Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) and gallbladder diseases is still unclear and is controversial. We conducted a retrospective study to clarify the prevalence of gallbladder diseases, and factors related to gallbladder diseases, and relationships between H. pylori infection, gallstones, cholecystitis and cholecystic polypus.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The retrospective study was performed at the Aerospace Center Hospital in Beijing. The subjects in this study were a healthy population who underwent health examinations at the hospital between 2012 and 2015. The logistic regression models were used to explore the relationships between H. pylori infection and gallbladder diseases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were 7803 (43.4%) subjects with H. pylori infection, 995 (5.5%) with gallstones, 219 (1.2%) with cholecystitis and 1003 (5.6%) with cholecystic polypus amongst 17,971 subjects, respectively. In subjects aged 45 years or less, the prevalence of gallstones in the H. pylori (+) group was lower than that in the H. pylori (-) group (OR=0.653; 95%CI: 0.468-0.911; P=0.012). The prevalence of cholecystic polypus in the H. pylori (+) group was significantly higher than that in the H. pylori (-) group (OR=1.160; 95%CI 1.012-1.328; P=0.033).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">H. pylori infection was related with cholecystic polypus and gallstones in a Chinese population.</AbstractText>
